    "Firstname": "Chris",

    "Narrative": "<p>12:45PM – 5:30PM – Worker responded to the ER and met with Sgt. Flynn.&nbsp;Lawrence Towles, Forensic Investigator for the Office of the Chief Medical Examiner was also present for the interviews of the parents.&nbsp;&nbsp;Police were contacted by a neighbor of the family at 11:05am and responded&nbsp;to the residence as well as EMT.&nbsp;</p><p><br></p><p>The parents reported to CPD, that Theo was put in his crib around 10:30PM on 9/11/2020.&nbsp;Theo was heard crying around 5:30AM.&nbsp;He went into Theo’s room, gave him his pacifier, Theo calmed down and laid Theo down.&nbsp;It is unknown if the father picked Theo up and placed him face down.&nbsp;While at the Hospital, Mr. Bissonette made a statement, “Its all my fault.” Then stated he was playing X box and drinking Jack Daniels all night. Police also found marijuana in the home and are unsure if he was using at the time of the incident.&nbsp;</p><p><br></p><p><br></p><p>CPD Case # 200054829</p><p>Medical Examiner Case # 20-12717</p><p><br></p><p>Ms. Naumann and Mr. Bissonette were interviewed by this worker, Sgt. Flynn, and Mr. Towles outside of the facility due to COVID restrictions.&nbsp;&nbsp;The parents had several family members at the ER with them providing emotional support to her.&nbsp;</p><p><br></p><p>The parents provided their demographic information to Mr. Towles, Sgt. Flynn and this worker.&nbsp;Mr. Bissonette is employed at Focus Construction as a carpenter for the last 2 ½ years and has a high school education.&nbsp;He denied any history of maltreatment (adult of child).&nbsp;He reported he smokes marijuana recreationally daily.&nbsp;Ms. Naumann has a medical marijuana card and he smokes her marijuana. He stated he will not get a medical marijuana card because that would prohibit him from owning firearms.&nbsp;He reported he drinks alcohol on the weekends, but not on Sunday, because he has not gone to work on Monday.&nbsp;</p><p><br></p><p>Ms. Naumann reported she was employed at the Olive Garden prior to the COVID pandemic and has not been back to work since.&nbsp;(She also gave birth during that time).&nbsp;She was a catering and delivery specialist,&nbsp;she gave indication that she plans to return to work at some point, she worked there for 4 ½ to 5 yrs.&nbsp;&nbsp;She reported receiving unemployment for her income.&nbsp;She has some college education and did not specify.&nbsp;She reported using medicinal marijuana for anxiety and to stimulate her appetite.&nbsp;She denied any other concerns or medications.&nbsp;She also stated she will drink occasionally “when she can” citing caring for Theo as why she can’t drink alcohol “a lot.”&nbsp;</p><p><br></p><p>Ms. Naumann is the primary caregiver for Theo as she is home all day with him while the father works.&nbsp;They discussed sharing responsibilities with caring for Theo when Mr. Bissonette is home.&nbsp;&nbsp;Ms. Naumann stated she had one miscarriage in the past and Theo was her first baby.&nbsp;She began receiving prenatal care at about 4 weeks after she realized she was pregnant.&nbsp;She had anemia during pregnancy that was treated with over the counter Iron medication.&nbsp;She also reported she took anti-nausea medication and prenatal vitamins.&nbsp;She stated she smoked marijuana in the first month because she was nauseas and did not know she was pregnant.&nbsp;She then stopped.&nbsp;She stated she used CBD wax sometimes and herbal teas. She did not drink alcohol during her pregnancy.&nbsp;</p><p><br></p><p>Baby was last seen on 9/8/2020 at Bay Pediatrics by Brittany Easter, NP.&nbsp;The parents reported there were no concerns for Theo’s health at that visit.&nbsp;He was 13 lbs. and “some” ounces at the check.&nbsp;He could roll over, reach for objects, but couldn’t “grab” them with out assistance.&nbsp;He could lift his head and put his hand to his mouth (could “push his pacifier in his mouth”).&nbsp;He could also imitate sounds “try to talk.”&nbsp;</p><p><br></p><p>The family reported they did not smoke cigarettes or marijuana in the home.&nbsp;They reported using marijuana outside (back of home).&nbsp;Theo was 6.1 lbs. at birth.&nbsp;He was born on 5/7/2020 after she was induced&nbsp;on 5/6/2020.&nbsp;She was induced due to Theo measuring below the 1<sup>st</sup> percentile.&nbsp;He was born with Jaundice and received tight therapy for treatment.&nbsp;Other health concerns were that he was constipated a few days ago that was resolved, and he will sometimes “eat too much” and “choke” a little, (spit up).&nbsp;He has gotten 205 ml of children’s Tylenol for his teething pain.&nbsp;</p><p><br></p><p>In the last 24 hours, Theo got Similac Sensitive formula 6-8 oz every three hours.&nbsp;They tried baby food about a week ago and he didn’t like it.&nbsp;They did not notice any changes in his behaviors in the past 24 hours or prior as well.&nbsp;Mr. Bissonette stated he last fed Theo at about 10:30-11:00PM on 9/11/2020.&nbsp;Mr. Bissonette put his son in the crib around 11:30PM.&nbsp;Ms. Naumann then finished by “tucking him in.”&nbsp;The family was the only people in the home on this night.&nbsp;</p><p><br></p><p>At 5:30AM Mr. Bissonette got up to go to the bathroom (between the rooms), and then heard Theo crying.&nbsp;He went into the room, put Theo’s “binky” back in his mouth.&nbsp;Theo rolled on his right side and was laying on his side, hands by his head on his binky, looking forward with his head in a neutral position.&nbsp;He rubbed Theo’s back three times and Theo fell back asleep.&nbsp;</p><p><br></p><p>They have a crib that can transition into a toddler bed, currently it is a crib.&nbsp;The parents have a camera that is focused on the bed, the feed they reported was a “live feed” and was not recorded anywhere.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;</p><p><br></p><p>At 10:30am, Ms. Naumann reported she went to check on Theo, and he was unresponsive.&nbsp;Theo was face down in the crib with a blanket “tangled” in a swaddling blanket that was in the bed.&nbsp;The parents also reported there was toys around the edge of the crib, as well as a plastic “star” toy (shines “stars” on the ceiling”).&nbsp;Two pillows were “stuffed” between the crib mattress and side due to Theo beginning to roll and “wiggle.”&nbsp;Ms. Naumann stated she feared he would get his leg or arm caught in the crib and it would cause injury.&nbsp;There was a fitted sheet and another “quilted” blanket reported in the crib.&nbsp;Mr. Bissonette reported he had moved the blankets down to the end of the crib, out of reach of Theo, prior to leaving the room at 5:30.&nbsp;When Theo was found by Ms. Naumann, the swaddling blanket was underneath Theo, face down, with his hands by his neck/face.&nbsp;Face to the left.&nbsp;She only saw some “slobber” on his face.&nbsp;</p><p><br></p><p>Ms. Naumann then stated she screamed, grabbed Theo, tried to “shake him awake.”&nbsp;He was “ice cold and pale.”&nbsp;She ran outside screaming with Theo in her arms.&nbsp;Neighbors came out of their homes.&nbsp;A “random car” stopped in the road and they got out to try to help as well as a neighbor (name unknown, resides on the right side of the family’s home, when you are looking at the family’s home).&nbsp;Ms. Naumann went back inside the home.&nbsp;The neighbor called 911 (as well as she reported others were too), the neighbor was on the phone with dispatch who was giving instructions for CPR.&nbsp;Ms. Naumann and the neighbor tried CPR. Mr. Bissonette then said he heard the ambulance coming and he “grabbed him (Theo) and started running to meet the ambulance.&nbsp;EMT staff retrieved the baby and immediately left the scene to transport Theo to the ER where he was pronounced dead.&nbsp;Ms. Naumann stated she did try CPS as well as tried to see if he had any blockages in his throat.&nbsp;</p><p><br></p><p>Both parents were visibly upset.&nbsp;They made statements that blamed themselves for the incident.&nbsp;&nbsp;Mr. Bissonette stated he should have removed the blanket. Ms. Naumann stated several times she is a very light sleeper and didn’t hear anything from her son.&nbsp;Mr. Bissonette is reported to be a very heavy sleeper.&nbsp;Mr. Bissonette also stated, “I should have just picked him up and brought him in with us.”&nbsp;</p><p><br></p><p>Ms. Naumann stated she went to bed around 1:30AM.&nbsp;She stated she last smoked marijuana around 3:30PM that day.&nbsp;Mr. Bissonette stated he went to bed about a half hour after Ms. Naumann. He last drank at around 12:30am, reported to have 4-5 Jack and Cokes while playing Xbox.&nbsp;He also reported smoking marijuana on that date.&nbsp;&nbsp;The parents denied use of any other illegal or prescription drugs or abusing them.&nbsp;</p><p><br></p><p>This worker spoke to a friend/fictive parent of Ms. Naumann’s.&nbsp;She stated she was concerned for Ms. Naumann’s mental health.&nbsp;Worker discussed grief counseling for the family and offered any assistance with obtaining it.&nbsp;They reported they will contact this worker with any needed assistance.&nbsp;This worker also provided Mobile Crisis number to the family if needed. They were also encouraged to come to the ER if needed.&nbsp;Ms. Naumann has made comments to her family about “I don’t know how I’m gong to make it.”&nbsp;Worker encouraged the family to utilize mobile crisis or 911 if needed.&nbsp;Worker also encouraged the parents to not engage in illegal drug use or to “drink” to excess as it may further esclate their current moods and feelings.&nbsp;&nbsp;The fictive mother also stated, “I am worried about the pills.”&nbsp;She then stated, “Not that they are taking any now.”&nbsp;She did not elaborate on the statement.&nbsp;She then stated Ms. Naumann has had a “hard life.”&nbsp;Citing her “having to get away from her father and step mother as well a former boyfriend dying and having a miscarriage.&nbsp;Worker encouraged the family to obtain counseling and again offered assistance.&nbsp;This worker also offered the family any support the agency could provide and possible assistance with burial costs if needed.&nbsp;The family gave indication that they would be able to provide funds for burial.&nbsp;</p><p><br></p><p>This worker, Sgt. Flynn and Mr. Towles responded to the family’s residence.&nbsp;CPD and Mr. Towles recovered the bedding and other items in the home to send to the medical examiner’s office. While in the home, drug paraphernalia was found.&nbsp;CPD retrieved two rolled up one dollar bills with while residue, two small&nbsp;pieces of aluminum foil, and other drug paraphernalia that did not appear to be associated with medical marijuana.&nbsp;&nbsp;There was medical marijuana containers throughout the home.&nbsp;There was indication that the family may have smoked marijuana in the home (unknown if child was in room/home) due to observations of implements used to “unclog” pipes, and “roaches” (marijuana) found in the home.&nbsp;There was also an empty bottle of Lord Calvert on the kitchen counter and a half bottle of Jack Daniels, as well as half a 20 oz Coke.&nbsp;Sgt. Flynn stated patrol reported Mr. Bissonette stated he “drank the whole bottle” referring to the Lord Calvert.&nbsp;Pictures were taken of the crib, sleeping area as well as the drug paraphernalia that would seized by CPD, worker also took pictures of the condition of the home.&nbsp;Worker also observed a back-porch area that had evidence that the parents did smoke marijuana on the back porch.&nbsp;There was paraphernalia and marijuana residue on a counter as well as medical marijuana containers.&nbsp;</p><p><br></p><p>The home had some clutter, however, there were no observable safety concerns during this visit.&nbsp;There are no other children in the home.&nbsp;</p><p><br></p><p>Worker was unable to complete referral in CJAMS on 9/12/2020 due to an inability to search and add family members to the referral.  Worker was able to complete referral on 9/13/2020.  </p><p><br></p>",

